WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:09.099
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: OK, Paul, money.

00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:13.650
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Thank you. I can offer the
Board three for the price of one today. I

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.770
have got three items, really, to subsume within
the finance report. You do not often get that

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.759
much value from a finance director, but there
you go. Try it for luck.

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:23.609
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: We will judge that in a minute.

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:27.599
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: The first one I cannot find
a direct link to finance with, but I have

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:31.050
been asked to bring it to the Board anyway,
so let me deal with that. It is a procedural

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:31.550
matter.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.480
Members of the Board will be aware that, as
part of the creation of NHS Improvement, the

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.269
patient safety function that we currently
perform in large measure is transferring to

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:51.589
NHSI, and we have taken urgent action to kick
that off in legal form between these two meetings,

00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:56.390
in order to keep the timetable flowing through
to 1st April.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.189
The urgent action was carried out by the Chair,
the Chief Executive and two of the non-executive

00:01:00.189 --> 00:01:03.479
directors, so my purpose in bringing this
to the Board is merely to let you know that

00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:10.220
that has happened and to invite you to note
it. Essentially the reason that the Board

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:16.720
is involved in this is that this is transferring
a legal function of NHS England, by agreement

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.890
with NHS Improvement, in the legal form of
the Trust Development Authority.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:28.110
It is a transfer of that legal responsibility,
and clearly a measure which does that sort

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.720
of thing needs to be with the sanction of
the Board. So I am sure there are many around

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.110
the table who can tell you all the details
of that if you are excited by knowing the

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:41.229
legal details of it, but for now I ask you
just to note it, and I will move on to finance.

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:47.390
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Just before you do, are there
any questions? That is a transfer, not a delegation?

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.740
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: It is. It is absolutely clearly
so: a transfer of a legal function, in agreement

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.790
with the DH, I should have said, who clearly
need to agree all of these sorts of things.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.909
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: From a clinical perspective,
fine. Okay.

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:04.820
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Just on what Paul said, it
will be the Department of Health, through

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.229
the Secretary of State, that will be directing
the Trust Development Authority to exercise

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:16.189
our 13R duty, and they will be producing legal
regulations to that effect, so this is the

00:02:16.189 --> 00:02:17.599
Department of Health in the driving seat on
this.

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:23.279
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. So, from our perspective
though, we are happy to note this? Thank you.

00:02:23.279 --> 00:02:27.620
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Okay, so turning to my more
conventional slot, if there is such a thing,

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.519
the month nine finances that you have in front
of you. I will keep it brief this month, because

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:35.529
for once I stole my own thunder last time
by telling you what I was going to tell you

00:02:35.529 --> 00:02:36.049
this month.

00:02:36.049 --> 00:02:39.739
It is normally the Chief Executive who steals
my thunder before I get to it, but I thought

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:45.579
I would get in first last month. Also, many
colleagues around this table will have spent

00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:50.209
a happy few minutes this morning at the Commissioning
Committee, which has now taken over the oversight

00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:54.709
of financial delivery in year - and, indeed,
financial planning - from the Investment Committee,

00:02:54.709 --> 00:02:58.779
so a number of us have spent this morning
going through this in rather more detail anyway.

00:02:58.779 --> 00:03:04.150
For now, I will restrict myself to just pointing
out a few of the more significant movements

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.760
between month eight and month nine. You might
care to have appendix A in front of you to

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.960
refer to.

00:03:10.809 --> 00:03:15.719
In there you will see that the net overspend
on CCGs has reduced from £51 million last

00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:22.400
month to £22 million this month, which is,
frankly, pretty much a point landing for a

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:28.829
multibillion-pound - indeed, close to £60
billion - sector of commissioning.

00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:34.889
The improvement is largely due to 18 additional
CCGs in the context of the month nine stocktake,

00:03:34.889 --> 00:03:41.739
which I talked to you about last time, identifying
upsides, which brings the total of CCGs reporting

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:44.590
positive variances this year to 35.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:51.519
That is about an £85 million positive variance
from those CCGs, predominantly located in

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:57.669
the North and in London. At the other end
of the spectrum we have 27 CCGs doing worse

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:02.930
than planned, with a total overspend between
them of £121 million. I am pleased to say

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.480
that number has not grown significantly through
the stocktake, so that at least is a good

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.339
sign.

00:04:08.339 --> 00:04:12.459
The second good sign is that the regional
specialised commissioning position has not

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:18.780
changed materially between the months. On
the other hand, we do have to recognise now,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:24.599
I am afraid, a higher overspend on the Cancer
Drugs Fund than was assumed at month eight.

00:04:24.599 --> 00:04:29.940
We now think the overspend in the forecast
is £100 million, and I have to tell you there

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:34.500
it probably a further risk against that of
between £20 million and £40 million, which

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:38.830
I will be able to confirm when we talk next
time.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:42.370
If all of that came to pass, we could have
spending on the Cancer Drugs Fund this year

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:47.900
of anything up to about £480 million against
the budget of £340 million, which clearly

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.169
is a further emphasis that needs to be put
on the discussion that we had earlier this

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:56.310
afternoon, because that level of overspend
on an already increased fund is clearly not

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.150
sustainable going forward.

00:04:58.789 --> 00:05:04.210
Finally, underspends in other areas of direct
commissioning, central programme costs and

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:09.210
our old friend the legacy continuing healthcare
settlements have all increased. The underspends

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:13.479
have increased, and that is good news, as
I indicated they would in my presentation

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.139
last month. There is no significant new news
there.

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:22.220
Overall, what you have in front of you is
a core forecast of £295 million underspend

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:27.409
in this year, with likely upsides taking that
further, as I point out in the report, in

00:05:27.409 --> 00:05:33.169
the latter part of it, which would take it
just over the £400 million line; in line

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:37.909
with the projection which I shared with you,
if you recall, last time at this meeting.

00:05:37.909 --> 00:05:42.830
I would repeat my caveat that I made last
time, that within that £400 million there

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:47.830
are significant once-off benefits from the
central programme cost, underspends and the

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:52.379
historic continuing healthcare settlements
in particular which will simply not be available

00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:56.539
to us next year, because we have obviously
adjusted those budgets going forward and part

00:05:56.539 --> 00:06:02.189
of our strategy for allocations in 2016-17
was to reduce the amount that we spend on

00:06:02.189 --> 00:06:06.229
our central programmes in order to maximise
the amount that is available for a combination

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:09.319
of front-line services and transformation.

00:06:09.319 --> 00:06:15.719
So, underspends this year, but do not read
too much into availability for next year.

00:06:15.719 --> 00:06:21.789
However, they do enable us, for now, to make
a bigger and, frankly, much-needed contribution

00:06:21.789 --> 00:06:26.960
to an overall health sector position which,
as I commented last month, is not really yet

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:33.460
showing the full effects of the NHS Improvement-led
interventions which we discussed in order

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:37.379
to bring the overspends in the provider sector
down.

00:06:37.379 --> 00:06:41.050
In that context, clearly the system is going
to have to pull out all the stops as we head

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:47.099
into the year-end to achieve the required
overall financial balance by the end of the

00:06:47.099 --> 00:06:52.500
year. We have already, as it were, put £400
million of underspend into that position.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:58.419
We will continue to look for further opportunities
to make a positive contribution to that challenge,

00:06:58.419 --> 00:07:03.349
and I will be able to report more on that
when we get to the March meeting.

00:07:03.349 --> 00:07:06.699
That is really all I wanted to say on in-year
finances. As I said, there is nothing much

00:07:06.699 --> 00:07:12.039
that I have just said that I did not tell
you last month in the report. Maybe I will

00:07:12.039 --> 00:07:16.300
pause at that point, just in case there are
any questions on that, before I come to the

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:20.129
surprise third act of this particular play.

00:07:20.129 --> 00:07:25.280
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think I would just reiterate
your comment about how the fact that there

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:34.860
is a surplus this year should not be seen
as being any indication that this is about

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:36.530
significant underspends.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.430
It is about the way in which a number of the
provisioning and other things have worked

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:47.349
through. Also, a number of things that have
worked this year will not work next year,

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:52.270
and therefore we need to be ready and thinking
about that, as I know you are now.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:54.099
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Yes, indeed.

00:07:54.099 --> 00:07:59.210
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. Any other comments?
No? Thank you, Paul. Item three.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:03.819
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Item three is just that, having
told you this time what I told you last time,

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:06.240
I am going to tell you this time what I am
going to tell you next time.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.490
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: You were called Mystic Meg
earlier today, so continue your record.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.460
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: Yes. I am not sure I like
that description, but, anyway, let's carry

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:15.960
on.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:20.340
This is really looking forward into 2016-17.
I have not got a report on 2016-17 planning

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.370
to bring to this particular meeting, because
it is a little early.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:29.039
We have had a submission from CCGs and NHS
Improvement, with whom we are working hand

00:08:29.039 --> 00:08:33.380
in hand, as it were, on this particular issue.
Similarly, from the providers. We have got

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:37.620
a further submission in a couple of weeks'
time, so I do anticipate that when we meet

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.350
together in March I will be able to give you
a more substantive update.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.370
It is worth saying that the big focus that
we have at the moment is getting activity

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:50.720
right in 2016-17. It is clearly the thing
that underpins the integrity of the forecasting,

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.750
and it has frankly been a problem for the
NHS in previous years in really getting a

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.470
firm grip at that stage on activity.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:03.300
We have provided an awful lot more support
to the system this year, in order to get proper

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:08.160
trend analysis into the system and a proper
understanding of what drives, in particular,

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.220
the waiting time metrics, and how to plan
the activity for that. We have worked closely

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.500
with CCGs to make sure that process is working
well.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:22.290
We clearly have more work to do on that, and
I hope the March submissions will take us

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:27.150
in that direction. It is always a big challenge,
of course, to make sure that the plans of

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:31.310
commissioners are directly related to the
plans of providers.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:37.480
Again, the significant deepening of the partnership
with NHS Improvement is leading to a good

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.920
progress of triangulation.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:45.550
The other feature that we are working closely
with NHSI on is the Sustainability and Transformation

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:51.350
Fund, which is the way in which the funding
for the structural deficit in the provider

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.960
system is being deployed in 2016-17.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:10:00.480
Importantly, as you know, they have been working
on control totals and so on, which we have

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:06.010
worked on with them, and that is moving forward.
The bit that is probably most relevant for

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:10.300
us to work together on at the moment is the
performance trajectories, which are the important

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:15.290
second part of the conditionality around the
Sustainability Fund.

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:20.340
We are working very closely together to make
sure that the trajectories are realistic but

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:24.050
stretching, and get us back on track with
those of the performance metrics which are

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.240
not currently quite where they should be.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:31.410
Then of course, the third conditionality around
which we are spending a lot of time thinking

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:37.510
at the moment and producing the plans is the
longer-term planning that we initiate together

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:39.980
again in the spring.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:47.060
So, lots of work in setting up both the 2016-17
aspects of the Sustainability Fund and the

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:53.460
sustainability and transformation plans, which
will be an important part of that.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:57.980
I cannot clearly tell you yet how exactly
the financial balance is going to work out

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:58.960
in all of those things.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.380
It would be enormously surprising if the first
plans came in with all of the numbers that

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:07.750
we were expecting, but I hope when we next
meet I will be able to give you - with Richard,

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:12.100
of course, with whom we are working closely
to make sure there is an umbilical link, if

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:18.080
I can put it that way in this context, between
performance and activity and finance. The

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.410
process is in place; the results we will see
in March and April.

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:31.690
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Anything for Paul? More about
that subject at the next Board or two, I suspect.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.579
Thank you very much.

